1. Static Variables and methods:
A static variable is a variable that belongs to the class, not to a specific object. 
Only one copy of a static variable exists, shared by all objects of that class.

class Employee {
    static String company = "Microsoft";
    int empId;
    Employee(int empId) {
        this.empId = empId;
    }


    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

e.g.
System.out.println(Employee.company);
int result = Employee.add(5, 10);


Key Rules for static variables
- Declared using the static keyword
- Memory allocated once when the class is loaded
- Same value shared across all objects
- Accessed using class name (recommended)

Key Rules for static methods
- Can access static variables and methods directly
- Cannot access instance variables or instance methods directly
- Cannot use this or super
- Static Methods Cannot Access Instance Variables because..
  Instance variables depend on object creation, but static methods are loaded before objects exist. 
  Hence, Java prevents direct access.
